.TH GEFS 4
.SH NAME
gefs \- copy-on-write disk file system with snapshots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B disk/gefs
.B -r
.I user
.I file
.PP
.B disk/gefs
.B -c
.I file
.PP
.B mount -c {disk/gefs
[
.B -RS
] [
.BI -m " cachemb"
]
.IB file } " dir"
.PP
.B disk/gefs
[
.B -RS
] [
.BI -m " cachemb"
] [
.BI -M " aname" : dir
] ...
[
.BI -W " journal"
]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Gefs
is 9front's file system of that name, by Ori Bernstein, in Limbo.
It keeps a disk as a forest of copy-on-write B\(*e trees, so that
nothing on the disk is overwritten while it is in use:
a power cut or a crash leaves the disk as it was at the last commit,
and a block that comes back from the disk other than as it was written
is found by its hash and refused rather than served.
Any number of snapshots cost only the blocks that differ.
The disk format is 9front's, byte for byte: a disk made by either
is read and written by the other.
.PP
The
.I file
is usually a disk partition, such as
.B #S/nvme0p3
on the Raspberry Pi 5;
it may be any file big enough.
.B -r
reams (makes) an empty file system on it, owned by
.IR user ,
and exits.
.B -c
checks it and exits, saying
.B ok
or
.B "not ok"
on the last line of a report on standard error;
the exit status says the same.
Otherwise
.I gefs
serves the file system with 9P on its standard input, for
.IR mount (1),
or, with
.BR -M ,
mounts it itself, once for each
.BI -M " aname" : dir
given, and returns.
.PP
Only one server may have a disk open: two would wreck it.
.B -M
is the way to have it on several directories at once, as 9front's
posted service is mounted more than once;
the attach name
.I aname
picks what is seen:
.TF dump
.TP
.B main
the file system (the default);
.TP
.B adm
the users file,
.BR /adm/users ,
and the control files
.B ctl
and
.BR status ;
.TP
.B dump
every snapshot, by name, read only;
.TP
.I label
the snapshot or fork of that name: read only, or writable if it is a fork.
.PD
.PP
The user named at attach must be in
.BR /adm/users ,
whose lines are
.IB id : name : leader : members\fR,
as in 9front.
A reamed disk has
.BR adm ,
.B none
and
.IR user .
There is no authentication; the user is the attach's.
.PP
Writes are committed every five seconds, and by
.B sync
below.
A commit has four steps with a device flush between each,
.B flush
on
.IR sd (3)'s
.B sdctl
when the
.I file
is under
.BR #S ,
so that a drive's write cache cannot put them out of order.
A commit that fails part way leaves the file system read only,
and the disk as it was at the commit before.
.PP
Writing a line to
.B ctl
in the
.B adm
tree controls it; the write returns when the command is done:
.TF "retain snap spec"
.TP
.B sync
commit now.
.TP
.B halt
commit and stop serving.
.TP
.B check
check the file system as
.B -c
does, while it is in use;
the write fails if the check does.
.TP
.B users
read
.B /adm/users
again.
.TP
.BI snap " old new"
make a read-only snapshot
.I new
of the tree
.IR old .
.TP
.BI fork " old new"
make a writable copy
.I new
of
.IR old .
.TP
.BI delsnap " name"
delete the snapshot
.IR name ,
giving back the blocks only it held.
.TP
.BI retain " snap spec"
set how many of
.IR snap 's
automatic snapshots are kept:
.I spec
is a list of
.IB count @ unit\fR,
the unit being
.BR m ,
.B h
or
.B d
(minutes, hours, days), and optionally a number before it,
for one every so many
.RB ( 12@2h );
a count left out keeps them all, and 0 takes none;
.B default
restores the default,
.BR "60@m 24@h @d" .
.TP
.BI usereserve " n"
let writes use the reserve kept at the end of each arena (1) or not (0).
.PD
.PP
The automatic snapshots are named
.IB tree @ unit . date\fR,
for instance
.BR main@hour.2026.11.05_08:00:04 ,
and are seen in
.BR dump .
Reading
.B status
reports the arenas, their space, the snapshots and their labels.
.PP
The options are:
.TP
.B -R
read only.
.TP
.B -S
anyone may attach to
.BR adm .
.TP
.BI -m " cachemb"
the size of the block cache, in megabytes (default 64).
.TP
.BI -W " journal"
write every block written, and every device flush, to the file
.IR journal ,
for
.B tests/host/gefs_crash_test.sh
to make from it the disks a power cut could leave.
It is not for use otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Make a file system on the Pi 5's third NVMe partition,
mount it, and take a snapshot:
.IP
.EX
disk/gefs -r inferno '#S/nvme0p3'
disk/gefs -M main:/n/gefs -M adm:/n/gefs.adm -M dump:/n/dump '#S/nvme0p3'
echo snap main before-upgrade > /n/gefs.adm/ctl
ls /n/dump
.EE
.PP
Get a file back from an hour ago:
.IP
.EX
cp /n/dump/main@hour.2026.11.05_08:00:04/lib/profile /n/gefs/lib/profile
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/disk/gefs
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR kfs (4),
.IR sd (3),
.IR mount (1)
.br
.IR "GEFS, A Good Enough File System" ,
Ori Bernstein, in 9front's
.BR /sys/doc/gefs.ms .
.SH BUGS
One process serves everything in turn, where 9front's runs readers
in parallel with the writer: a long read holds up the rest.
.br
There is no authentication.
.br
The Pi cannot yet boot from it; the boot partition stays FAT.
